text=['# P3A: implementation audit and bounded mechanism diagnosis','', '**Conclusion:** the tested implementation checks pass. CV2 reduces conditional gradient noise at most tested reference locations, but has strong local reversals and rare-observation concentration. These results support a nonuniform variance-reduction mechanism; three exact-stream replays show large stochastic updates at failure. They do not establish that ordinary per-step Hessian overhead alone explains the time-to-accuracy loss.','', '## Implementation and cost audit','',f"- {summary['audit_checks']} fixed-input gradient/step cases passed against standalone NumPy float64 and direct formulas, at two step sizes per case. Includes anchor and 16 independent reference locations, all six minibatch sizes, CV1/CV2/ULA.",f"- Maximum absolute gradient disagreement: {max(x['max_gradient_absolute_error'] for x in audit['checks']):.3g}; maximum step disagreement: {max(x['max_step_absolute_error'] for x in audit['checks']):.3g}.",'- CV1/CV2 P arrays are identical. Both agree with independent inverse posterior Hessian and the standalone P1 fixed-metric construction; L L^T=P. Likelihood Hessian is unregularized for Taylor correction; prior and ridge enter only where specified.','- P1 GPU replication and P2 locked core hashes agree. Algebra equality is established with identical fixed inputs; NumPy/JAX RNG sequences are not claimed identical.','- 296 run timing identities checked: warm = historical anchor attribution + locally measured setup + synchronized sampling; cold adds shape compilation. Evaluation, functionals and checkpoint I/O are excluded for both methods. Cold is not fresh-process cold timing.','- Existing setup records aggregate Hessian construction, inversion and factorization into cache_metric_seconds. Those components cannot be split retrospectively. Full-H CV1 also needs the Hessian, and CV2 reuses it; attributing the whole Hessian setup uniquely to CV2 would be wrong.','', '## Exact finite-population gradient noise','',r'For residual vectors $r_i$, uniform sampling without replacement gives $E\|P^{1/2}(\hat g-g)\|^2=\frac{N^2(N-n)}{n(N-1)}\frac1N\sum_i\|(r_i-\bar r)L\|^2$, with $P=LL^T$ and row-vector convention. CV1 residual is $x_i[p_i(\theta)-p_i(\hat\theta)]$; CV2 additionally subtracts $x_i w_i(\hat\theta)x_i^T(\theta-\hat\theta)$. Deterministic anchor, Hessian and prior terms cancel from conditional noise.','', 'All observations are enumerated. Centered-vector calculation agrees with independently formed covariance-matrix trace. Anchor noise is zero for both methods. Batch size changes the common finite-population factor, so at fixed theta the CV2/CV1 ratio is independent of n; sampling trajectories can still depend on n.','',f"**Primary fixed reference locations (16):** CV2 lower at {g['cv2_lower_count']}/16. Median CV2/CV1 ratio {g['median_ratio']:.4f}; range {g['min_ratio']:.4f}–{g['max_ratio']:.3f}. Ratio of location-mean noise levels {g['mean_cv2_preconditioned_mse']/g['mean_cv1_preconditioned_mse']:.3f}. The median must not be described as a 50x overall noise reduction: the mean is much less favorable.",'', '|Location group|Locations|Median ratio|Maximum ratio|CV2 lower|','|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
